// Lore for the Ghyran map (the published Everspring Swathe plate).
// See src/data/lore/index.js.
//
// Ghyran is not a garden. It is a realm where everything grows too fast and
// nothing stops, and where Nurgle got his hands into the soil early enough
// that half the Swathe’s place-names are anatomical or diseased. Entries say
// what a place is, who holds it, and why an army would march there. Where the
// published lore is thin — and for most of these names it is nothing but a
// label on a map — the entry says what the cartography itself shows.
export const GHYRAN_LORE = {
  'ghyran': `The Realm of Life, and the realm every other power in the Mortal Realms wants a share of, because Ghyran feeds them. Its Jade Kingdoms grow food, timber and Aqua Ghyranis in quantities no other realm can match, and its rivers carry that abundance to realmgates that carry it everywhere else.

The plate here is the Everspring Swathe, the realm’s heartland: Aquia and Thyria in the west, Verdia and Erosia across the middle, and the long rotting reach south through Yska, Humidia and Quogmia to the Amnios Sea. Nurgle’s Garden was planted in this ground during the Age of Chaos and never wholly dug out, which is why a map of the Realm of Life reads like a physician’s chart.

Alarielle’s Sylvaneth hold the deep woods, the Cities of Sigmar hold the rivers and the gates, the Maggotkin hold the swamps, and none of them has held any of it for long.`,

  // --- Aquia ---------------------------------------------------------------
  'aquia': `The north-western Jade Kingdom, a country of pale lakes, peat and slow water where the land is barely higher than the sea that surrounds it. Aquia grows well and drains badly, and every road across it is a causeway somebody has to maintain.

Its value is water rather than ground: the springs that feed Aquia’s lakes are clean, which in the Realm of Life is rarer and worth more than soil.`,

  'electria-sea': `The cold open water off Aquia’s western shore, named for the storms that crack across it out of a clear sky. Fleets cross it because the alternative is the long southern route past Decrepita, and enough of them arrive to keep the crossing in use.`,

  'creeping-nail': `A long thin spit of land reaching north from Aquia into the sea, silting further out every season. Fishing camps sit along it and move when it moves, which is often enough that nobody bothers building in stone.`,

  'the-sog': `The waterlogged shoulder of northern Aquia, where the rivers give up on reaching the sea in any organised way and simply spread. Armies that try to cross it lose their wagons first and their tempers shortly after.`,

  'lake-vigour': `A broad pale lake in Aquia’s interior whose water is potent enough to be bottled and sold, the closest thing this kingdom has to a treasury. Whoever holds the shoreline holds the drawing rights, and the shoreline has changed hands more than once on that account.`,

  'lake-crud': `The smaller, fouler twin of Lake Vigour, a few leagues east and downhill of it. What goes into the Vigour clean comes out here as something the local villages have learned to boil twice, and they still drink it, because it is what there is.`,

  'lake-triad': `Three linked pools in southern Aquia, coiled around one another in a spiral the land itself seems to have drawn. Wizards argue about whether the shape is natural; the villagers who fish the pools are more interested in the fact that all three flood together.`,

  'jutting-nail': `A blunt headland on Aquia’s south-western coast, the last solid rock before the Electria Sea. It is a landmark for anything sailing north and a beacon-station for anyone who wants early warning of what is coming.`,

  'shrug-slough': `A shallow marsh-lake on the border between Aquia and Thyria, too wide to go round and too soft to cross. Both kingdoms claim the fishing and neither garrisons it, which suits the smugglers who work the reeds.`,

  'mistral-peaks': `The ridge that divides Aquia’s lowlands from Thyria’s river country, swept by winds that come off the western sea and drop nothing but cold. The passes are few, and the tolls charged on them are the closest thing the border has to law.`,

  'the-spurn': `A pale flooded basin in southern Aquia, cut off from the sea by a lip of higher ground. What flows in does not flow out, which has concentrated the water into something valuable and slightly wrong.`,

  'geet-lake': `A narrow, deep water on Aquia’s western edge, sunk into the rock rather than spread across it. Divers work it for what the lake preserves, and the local families are careful not to say what that is to outsiders.`,

  'hauntcave-coast': `The undercut southern shore of Aquia, riddled with sea-caves that flood and drain twice a day and make noises doing it. Wreckers used to work this coast; whether they still do depends on who you ask and how far from the caves you ask them.`,

  // --- The Greenglades -----------------------------------------------------
  'kingdom-of-the-greenglades': `A river kingdom on the green peninsula north of Thyria, holding the Viscus valley and the coast either side of it. It is small, fertile and well placed, which in Ghyran means it has been invaded roughly once a generation and has become very good at rebuilding.

The Greenglades sell timber and grain north across the Sea of Elemental Truths and take payment in Azyrite steel. That trade is the kingdom’s strength and its standing risk: it makes the Greenglades worth defending to powers who would otherwise never look at them.`,

  'sea-of-elemental-truths': `The wide northern water between the Greenglades and the drowned isthmus, named for the way Ghyran’s life-magic runs raw and undiluted through it. Ships crossing report the sea telling them things; captains have learned to sail it without listening too hard.`,

  'tiara': `A small crowned isle in the northern sea, ringed by shallows that break a keel before a lookout sees them. It is used as a waymark and a burial ground, in that order of importance to the living.`,

  'slidecrown-isle': `A long low island west of the Greenglades, its whole spine sliding slowly into the sea. Settlements on it are built to be dismantled, and the islanders measure a house’s age in how many times it has been moved.`,

  'heldenhammers-triumph': `A monument on the Greenglades coast marking a victory won in Sigmar’s name, raised where the invaders were finally broken against the shore. It is a rallying point as much as a memorial, and the Freeguilds of the Greenglades muster beneath it by custom.

Whoever throws it down makes a point that carries further than the stone does, which is exactly why it is still standing and still guarded.`,

  'battle-of-the-white-krakens': `Open water off Slidecrown Isle where a fleet met the sea-beasts that gave the fight its name, and lost enough hulls to make the location worth marking. The wrecks are still down there, and so, according to every crew that has sailed over them, are the krakens.`,

  'viscus-river': `The thick, slow river that drains the Greenglades to the sea, silted to the consistency its name suggests. It is navigable by shallow craft and by nothing else, which makes control of the landings worth more than control of the water.`,

  // --- Thyria --------------------------------------------------------------
  'thyria': `A river-veined lowland thick with orchards that fruit twice a season under Ghyran’s restless growth, worked by free peoples who have learned to harvest fast and fortify faster. Its granaries feed half the Free Cities in the realm, which makes Thyria worth more standing than burned, a distinction not every raider observes.

Thyria is the male half of the twin Jade Kingdoms, bound to Verdia across the Triske Isles by a cycle of life old enough to predate the Free Cities that now farm its lowlands, its wildlife crossing the isles each season to meet Verdia’s in kind. Chaos wizards once seized the Triske Isles and turned them into the Squelchlobe Nations, slaughtering the Sylvaneth who guarded the crossing, and Thyria’s granaries have fed the war to win them back as readily as they feed anyone else.`,

  'living-city': `The Living City is grown rather than built, raised in a single working of Alarielle’s magic on the site of the Hollow Forest in Ghyran’s Everspring Swathe after an alliance of Sylvaneth, Stormcast Eternals and the Ghyran Guard drove out the plague-ridden Clan Morbidus. Fourteen days and nights of song-forged growth wove its walls from ironoak and thorn-vine, bastions that still visibly put down roots.

One of the three Seeds of Hope planted alongside Greywater Fastness and the Phoenicium, the city stands as Alarielle’s gift and her experiment both: since her own kindreds were judged too fey to keep watch over mortal gates, she granted the Living City to the free peoples on the understanding that they would tend the forest in return for its shelter. Sylvaneth glades still keep their own enclaves within the walls, wardens as much as tenants.

That arrangement has held for longer than most such bargains, and the Living City has become the clearest proof Order has that Sylvaneth and mortal ambition can share a wall without one devouring the other, so long as both sides remember whose forest it really is. It is exactly that success which makes the city worth taking: an enemy who could turn its groves against their keepers would be striking at the idea of the Seeds of Hope as much as at the city itself.`,

  'plaguespire': `A spire of fused, weeping stone on Thyria’s northern march, raised by Nurgle’s faithful as a shrine and left standing when the armies that built it were driven off. It has not fallen down, and nothing that grows near it is right.

Order patrols ride out to the spire, count the pilgrims camped around it, and ride back. Pulling it down properly would take a siege train and a season nobody has spared yet.`,

  'slumping-groves': `Orchard country north of Lake Innis where the trees grow faster than their own wood can bear and come down under the weight of their fruit. The harvest is enormous and the ground is treacherous, and Thyrian farmers consider both facts normal.`,

  'lake-innis': `A clean deep lake in northern Thyria, one of the few waters in the Swathe that has never needed boiling. The villages around it are richer than their size suggests and defend the shoreline accordingly.`,

  'lake-reflux': `A wide shallow lake on the Thyrian border that empties and refills on no schedule anyone has worked out, leaving its bed exposed for weeks at a time. Armies use the dry seasons as a road; the lake has drowned more than one column that misjudged them.`,

  'morbidus-warren': `The skaven undercity beneath Thyria, held by Clan Morbidus, whose plague-priests were driven out of the Hollow Forest when the Living City was grown over it. They did not go far. The warren is marked on the plate as full, which is a surveyor’s way of saying the diggings below are at capacity and something is going to have to come up.

Clan Morbidus breeds and brews rather than fights in the open, and the warren’s output goes into the wells and grain-stores of every settlement within a week’s tunnelling. Taking it would mean a campaign underground that no Freeguild has the stomach to begin, so instead the surface powers watch the entrances and burn what comes out.`,

  'silverwyr-gates': `A pass through the hills above the Living City, its approach flanked by rock the old kingdoms cut into gate-towers. The towers are ruins now and the pass is not, which makes this the obvious road for anything coming at the Living City overland.`,

  'vindpool': `A wind-scoured pool on the open ground east of Thyria, ringed by the stumps of a forest that was cleared generations ago and has not been allowed back. The clearance was deliberate; the reason for it has been forgotten by everyone except the Sylvaneth.`,

  'gloet-marsh': `A sucking marsh between Thyria’s orchards and the southern coast, impassable to wheels and unpleasant to everything else. It is the Living City’s western moat, and the city’s wardens take care that it stays as bad as it is.`,

  'extermination-wars': `Open ground east of the Living City where the campaign to clear Clan Morbidus out of the Hollow Forest was fought to its conclusion, and where the name stuck because of how thoroughly it was done. Sylvaneth, Stormcast and the Ghyran Guard fought here together, which is rarer than any of them admit.

The ground is good farmland now. It is also where every army that has since marched on the Living City has chosen to form up, because it is the last open space before the walls.`,

  'sludgemoot': `A river town on the Thyrian lowland, built where three channels of filth meet and where the barge traffic has to stop and transfer. Everything that moves through southern Thyria is handled here by somebody, and everybody takes their cut.`,

  'the-potence': `A river in western Thyria whose water runs thick with Ghyran’s raw life-magic, strong enough that crops on its banks grow to twice their proper size and kill the soil doing it. Farmers along it plant for one enormous year and then move on.`,

  'slicston': `A small trading settlement on Thyria’s southern coast, sat on the one firm landing between the marshes and the Dearth Coast. It has no walls worth the name and survives by being more useful to raiders as a market than as a target.`,

  'futilia-wood': `A wood on the southern Thyrian plain that has been cut down repeatedly and comes back within the decade, faster each time. The name is a woodsman’s joke that stopped being funny generations ago.`,

  'heartwound-lake': `A lake in western Thyria shaped like a wound that has not closed, fed by springs that run warm and slightly red. Nothing is definitively wrong with the water; nobody drinks it who has an alternative.`,

  'dearth-coast': `The barren southern shore of Thyria, where the soil runs out and the sea gives back nothing but weed. It is the one stretch of Thyrian coast an invader can land on without fighting for it, and the one stretch nobody wants.`,

  'kernelstone': `A ruined granary-town on the Thyrian plain, its stone silos still standing above a settlement that no longer exists. What emptied it is disputed; that the silos are dry and intact is not, and caravans still shelter in them.

A dry, defensible store on the road south is worth taking. Every army that comes this way stops here, which is the closest thing Kernelstone has to a future.`,

  // --- The Squelchlobe Nations ---------------------------------------------
  'squelchlobe-nations': `The Triske Isles as Chaos left them: a scatter of petty nations grown up on ground that once carried the seasonal crossing between Verdia and Thyria. A trio of Chaos wizards took the isles, killed the Sylvaneth wardens who kept the crossing open, and renamed what they had made.

The wizards are gone and the nations are not. They bicker, trade, and sell passage across the straits to whoever pays, and neither Jade Kingdom has been able to decide whether that is worth a war.`,

  'city-of-seven-bells': `A ruined port on the northern lip of the Globos Gulf, named for a peal that used to be rung across the water as a navigation aid. The bells are still in the tower and the tower is still standing; the city under it is not.

Whoever hangs a lookout in that tower sees everything that enters the gulf, which is why the ruin is contested far more often than a dead city should be.`,

  'globos-gulf': `The round enclosed water between Thyria and the Squelchlobe Isles, sheltered enough to anchor a fleet in and narrow enough at the mouth to be closed. Every power that has ever wanted the Triske crossing has started by wanting this.`,

  'chittersquirm-realmgate': `A gnawhole grown into something permanent on the Squelchlobe shore, its frame a knot of chewed stone and bone that skaven engineers keep open by main force. It does not lead anywhere its users advertise.

Clan traffic comes through it in bursts, floods the isles, and withdraws. Sealing it has been attempted twice; both attempts are commemorated by the fact that the gate is still there.`,

  'undergut': `A town dug into the sea-cliffs of the Squelchlobe Isles, more cellar than settlement, its warehouses cut back into the rock where the damp cannot reach them. Goods that need to stay dry in Ghyran are expensive to store, and Undergut charges accordingly.`,

  'vyras': `The largest of the Squelchlobe towns, sat on the strait where the old seasonal crossing used to run. Its council governs by auction: passage, anchorage and justice all go to whoever bids, and the town has grown fat on the arrangement.`,

  'bloth': `A fishing town on the southern Squelchlobe shore, working water so rich that the catch has to be culled rather than hunted. The people are well fed and heavily armed, the second being a consequence of the first.`,

  // --- The Gulf of Thorns --------------------------------------------------
  'gulf-of-thorns': `The great northern gulf that all but cuts Verdia off from the rotting country above it, studded with reef-thickets that give it its name. It is the fastest route between the eastern and western Swathe and the surest way to lose a hull.`,

  'gulf-of-hags': `The western arm of the northern water, a shallow, fog-bound gulf between Scorpion Tip and the Verdian shore. The fishing is good and the reputation is bad, and the fleets that work it are crewed by people with nowhere better to be.`,

  'epiglot-point': `The westernmost tip of the drowned northern isthmus, a bar of sand and grass that closes the throat of the gulf behind it. Watchtowers have stood here for as long as anyone has cared what comes down the coast.`,

  'scorpion-tip': `A hooked spit curling south from the isthmus, its point turned back on itself. Ships that shelter inside the hook are out of the weather and, more importantly, out of sight of anything passing.`,

  'coast-of-impossible-odds': `The northern Verdian shore, named by the survivors of a landing that should not have worked. It is a lee shore with no harbours, and every invasion of Verdia from the north has had to come ashore on it anyway.`,

  'the-quagmares': `A waterlogged plain on the far northern shore of the gulf, pocked with standing pools that never drain. Horses founder in it, which is how it is named, and armies that want the northern coast have to go round.`,

  'the-sludge': `The rotting northern headland above the Gulf of Thorns, where the land gives up being land by degrees. Nurgle’s work reached here first and left it; there is nothing to take and nobody contests it.`,

  'the-goblet': `A near-circular bay on the eastern gulf, almost closed by its own headlands and deep all the way in. It is the best natural harbour on this coast and the reason anyone holds the peninsula behind it.`,

  'hewing-peaks': `A hard grey range on the gulf’s eastern shoulder, quarried since the Age of Myth for stone that does not rot. In a realm where timber is eaten by its own fertility, that makes these peaks worth more than their height suggests.`,

  // --- Verdia --------------------------------------------------------------
  'verdia': `The broad, fertile heartland that cradles both the Athelwyrd and the Rotwater Blight, the truest expression of Ghyran’s abundance and its rot sharing the same soil. Whoever wants to claim the Realm of Life’s blessing, or cure its sickness, has to march through Verdia to do either.

Verdia is also half of a stranger inheritance: bound since the Age of Myth to its twin Thyria across the Triske Isles, the two Jade Kingdoms share a single cycle of life in which Verdia’s creatures are born female and Thyria’s male, with the wildlife of both crossing the isles each season to hunt and to breed. That bond has already been fought over once, when a trio of Chaos wizards conquered the isles outright and slaughtered their Sylvaneth wardens, and nothing about Verdia’s fertility has looked entirely safe since.`,

  'hammerhal-ghyra': `Hammerhal Ghyra is the life-realm half of the Twin-tailed City, grown on the Ghyran side of the Stormrift Realmgate in the fertile Jade Kingdom of Verdia. Where its twin smokes and hammers, Ghyra blooms: golden domes and celestial spires rise from streets that Ghyran’s restless growth threatens to reclaim if the gardeners ever stop cutting back.

It was raised in the same campaigns that built Hammerhal Aqsha, planted by the armies that followed Vandus Hammerhand out of the Realmgate Wars and fed from the first by Verdia’s abundance rather than by ore and coal. Alarielle’s blessing keeps its granaries full even as the city’s own hunger for timber and farmland presses harder every year against the Sylvaneth groves at its border.

The gate traffic that pays for Hammerhal Aqsha’s furnaces pays for Ghyra’s granaries just as surely, and the city’s fertility feeds Free Cities across Aqshy that could not otherwise be held. That dependence cuts both ways: anyone who wanted to starve the twin city, or seize the realmgate outright, would find Hammerhal Ghyra as essential a target as its smoke-wreathed other half, which is exactly why Sylvaneth wardens and Freeguild regiments alike still stand picket along its tree line.`,

  'greywater-fastness': `Greywater Fastness is an ironclad industrial stronghold in Ghyran’s Everspring Swathe, the second of the three Seeds of Hope raised after the Living City. Where its sister-seeds grew from Alarielle’s magic, Greywater Fastness was built, its furnaces and cannon foundries answering less to the Everqueen’s grace than to the vast cyclestone deposits beneath it.

It began as a scatter of walled villages and mines under Baroness Ciriline Zukaus, and became a true city only once Valius Maliti learned to draw power from realmstone, drawing aelven enchanters of the Eldritch Council and Ironweld engineers through the Festermere Realmgate to build it up in earnest. The expansion came at a cost: the deforestation and battlefield scarring of the Season of War left the Ghoul Mere in its wake and earned Greywater Fastness the lasting resentment of the Sylvaneth who once held that ground.

None of that has slowed its furnaces. Greywater Fastness arms the Free Peoples across every realm the gate network reaches, and the land scorched bare outside its walls is a trade its garrison still considers worth making, because a Seed of Hope that stops producing guns stops being useful to anyone, Sylvaneth grievance included.`,

  'phoenicium': `The Phoenicium is a fortress-sanctuary at the foot of the Arborean Mountain in Ghyran, third of the Seeds of Hope, its groves and amber statuary sheltering companies of Order between campaigns while its healers ask no questions of who they mend.

It began as Aelfgrove, buried whole beneath a tidal wave of sap during an Age of Myth eruption of the mountain, and lay sealed inside its own amber glacier until Stormcast Eternals, Sylvaneth and Azyrite settlers cleared and resettled it after the Realmgate Wars, renaming it for the phoenixes sacred to the grove it protects. In the wake of Nagash’s Necroquake, Nighthaunt legions swept over its garrison and haunted the population for a time before the gheists simply dissipated, a reminder that no Seed of Hope is ever quite as safe as its healers would like.

That open-handed mercy is the Phoenicium’s great strength and its standing risk in equal measure: wounded companies of Order recover here regardless of banner, which has made the sanctuary as valuable to Chaos infiltrators nursing their own wounds as it is to honest soldiers, and its wardens have learned to watch the grateful as closely as the grieving.`,

  'athelwyrd': `The Hidden Vale of Athelwyrd is Alarielle’s most sacred sanctuary, a refuge kept so deep within Ghyran that even her own Sylvaneth kin struggled to find it.

Alarielle fled here after the ruin of the Battle of Tears and the Last Hunt, only for the Grey Seer Skurrik to spy Gardus Steel Soul’s Hallowed Knights finding the way in and betray the secret to the Maggotkin. The Glottkin summoned Nurgle’s Deluge to flood the River Vitalis and force the passage during the Realmgate Wars, putting Alarielle to flight within her own last refuge, before she turned, reborn and wrathful, to help drive the brothers from the Genesis Gate and hurl the wounded Glottkin from the floating island herself.

That violation is why the Sylvaneth now guard Athelwyrd’s secrecy more jealously than any other ground in Ghyran, and why so few outsiders are ever shown the way in twice. A power that could find the Vale a second time would be striking at the Everqueen in the one place she once believed truly safe, which is reason enough that its wardens intend there never to be a second time.`,

  'rotwater-blight': `The diseased swamp that formed in Verdia’s Drydenwald when a fragment of the Oak of Ages Past crashed into Ghyran, the first ground in the realm to feel Nurgle’s touch. Its green fogs have corrupted land, water and even nearby realmgates ever since, spreading outward from the shimmering lake the Oak once shaded.

Stormcast Eternals and the surviving Sylvaneth of Ghyran fought through the Realmgate Wars to seal the Genesis Gate against Archaon’s reinforcements, and struck deeper still at the Battle of Rotwater, where they cut down Pupa Grotesse, the Great Unclean One whose corruption fed the river. Nurgle’s forces have never fully relinquished the Blight even so, and every attempt to cleanse it has only proven how deep the rot has spread.`,

  'oak-of-ages-past': `The fragment that fell into the Drydenwald and started the Rotwater Blight, and the stump that is left of it: a mass of dead heartwood the size of a hill, still standing where it came down. Nurgle’s servants treat it as a shrine and the Sylvaneth treat it as a grave.

It is the one fixed point in the Blight. Every campaign fought to cleanse the swamp is really a campaign to reach this, and every one of them has stopped short.`,

  'gates-of-dawn': `A ring of standing arches on the Verdian plain, aligned so that the sun comes through them on the first morning of the growing season. The rite performed there is older than the Cities of Sigmar and is still performed, by people who would rather not explain themselves.

The arches sit astride the road from Hammerhal Ghyra to the Blight. An army going south uses them as a muster point whether it believes in the rite or not.`,

  'lestermere-realmgate': `A realmgate on the Verdian lakeshore, small, well-mapped and heavily taxed. It is one of the two gates that keep Greywater Fastness supplied, and the Fastness garrisons it as if it were a wall of its own city.`,

  'gate-of-the-seventh-shard': `A realmgate standing in open Verdian country, named for the shard of realmstone set in its lintel. Six others are supposed to exist; nobody has produced them, and the wizards who go looking rarely come back with anything but theories.

Whatever it was built for, it works, and a working gate on the north Verdian road is worth a garrison regardless of its mysteries.`,

  'dhoshgar-fyrelodge': `A Fyreslayer magmahold sunk into the hills below Greywater Fastness, holding the only reliable source of forge-heat in a realm made of wet wood. The Dhoshgar lodge sells that heat, and its axes, to whoever brings ur-gold.

The Fastness and the lodge need each other and dislike each other in roughly equal measure. Neither has yet found a way to do without the other, which is the only reason the arrangement has lasted.`,

  'new-summercourt': `A city on Verdia’s northern neck, founded by settlers who lost an older Summercourt to the rot and rebuilt it here on higher, harder ground. It is the northern anchor of Verdian power and the first thing any invasion from the gulf has to break.

The court that gives it its name still sits, and still rules a good deal more coastline than its walls suggest.`,

  'the-neck': `The narrow isthmus joining northern Verdia to the main of the kingdom, a few leagues wide with water on both sides. Everything moving overland between the gulf coast and the Verdian heartland passes through it, and everyone who has ever held Verdia has fortified it.`,

  'the-pale-gorge': `A bleached canyon cut into northern Verdia by a river that no longer runs, its walls chalky and its floor dry. It is the only covered approach across the northern plain, and scouts on both sides know it.`,

  'verdigris': `A copper-working town on the north Verdian coast, its roofs and its people the same weathered green. The ore comes from the hills behind it and the finished metal goes out through the gulf.`,

  'slithid-jutt': `A low peninsula on Verdia’s northern shore, sloped so gently that the tide moves across a mile of it twice a day. Salt is panned here in quantity, which is worth fighting over in a realm where preserving food is harder than growing it.`,

  'naithwaites-crossing': `A ruined bridge-town on Verdia’s northern coast, sat on the ford that used to carry the coast road. The bridge is gone, the ford is not, and the ruins on either bank make good cover for whoever gets there first.`,

  'titansrest': `The ruin of a hold built into the bones of something enormous that died on the Verdian plain, its roof beams the creature’s own ribs. Whatever killed the titan left the bones, and whatever emptied the hold left those too.

Scavengers still work it. The bone is harder than oak and does not rot, which in Ghyran makes it a building material worth the risk.`,

  'scabrous-sprawl': `A ragged settlement spreading across the plain east of Titansrest, growing faster than anyone is governing it. Refugees from the Blight arrive here and stop, and the Sprawl absorbs them, which is the only thing about it that works.`,

  'fort-gardus': `A ruined Sigmarite fort on the Verdian march, raised during the Realmgate Wars and named for Gardus Steel Soul, who held the line here before the campaign moved on. It was abandoned rather than stormed, and it shows.

The walls are still walls. Any force pushing towards the Rotwater Blight from the north will want them, and the first army to spend a season rebuilding the place will have bought itself a very good position.`,

  'pickmanspire': `A tower-town on the Verdian plain, built vertically because the ground around it floods. Its upper floors are dry storage and its lower floors are not, and the whole settlement moves up and down with the season.`,

  'furtherfield': `A ruined farming settlement at the eastern edge of Verdia’s cultivated land, the last place the ploughs reached before the rot came back. The fields are still visible from a height, which is the only reason anyone knows how far the kingdom once ran.`,

  'nevergreen-peaks': `A range in central Verdia where nothing grows, an absence so total in the Realm of Life that pilgrims come to look at it. The bare rock is the cleanest ground in the Swathe and the only place a Nurgle contagion has never taken.

That makes the peaks a sanctuary and a strongpoint both. Armies that need to quarantine themselves come here, and the passes are held accordingly.`,

  'river-clot': `A river in western Verdia that runs slow and thick and occasionally stops altogether, backing up for weeks before it breaks through. The floods it produces are catastrophic and entirely predictable to anyone who watches the upstream marshes.`,

  'the-resurgence': `A river running east out of Verdia, named for the way it comes back: it sinks into the ground on the Verdian plain and rises again miles away on the Erosian side. The underground stretch is passable, and smugglers have been using it for longer than the Cities of Sigmar have existed.`,

  'breakers-lake': `A long narrow lake below Hammerhal Ghyra, whipped into a surf by winds funnelled down the valley. It supplies the twin city’s Ghyran half with water and is patrolled as heavily as any wall.`,

  'hardwon': `A ruined mining town in western Verdia, named by the people who dug it out of ground that did not want to give anything up. They got the ore and lost the town, which the survivors considered an even trade at the time.`,

  'boilslick': `A ruined settlement east of Hammerhal Ghyra, abandoned after the springs beneath it turned. The water still comes up hot and the buildings still stand, and nobody has gone back.`,

  'tundra-bubonicus': `A cold plague-scoured plain on Verdia’s southern edge, where Nurgle’s rot met ground too cold to grow in and settled for killing it instead. Nothing lives there; things cross it.`,

  'unquiet-graves': `Burial fields on the southern Verdian march, used for generations by the settlements the Blight has since swallowed. The dead there do not reliably stay put, and the Order garrisons that patrol the edge of the Rotwater Blight treat the graves as part of the same problem.`,

  // --- Erosia --------------------------------------------------------------
  'erosia': `The eastern kingdom beyond Verdia, a country of worn hills and river valleys where the rock is being taken apart faster than it is being made. Erosia’s soil is thin and its rivers are rich, and its people live along the water.

It is the land bridge between the Swathe’s heartland and the poisoned east, which means every army moving between the two has to cross it and none of them stays.`,

  'elmir-bay': `A sheltered bay on Erosia’s northern coast, ringed by dunes and shallow enough to beach a fleet on. It is the natural landing for anything coming into Erosia off the gulf, and Vineport keeps a watch on it for exactly that reason.`,

  'vineport': `Erosia’s working harbour, shipping the wine and preserved fruit that are the kingdom’s only reliable exports. It is small, walled, and considerably better defended than its size warrants, because everyone in Erosia understands what happens if it falls.`,

  'necrotic-edge': `The line on Erosia’s north-eastern coast where healthy ground stops and the dead ground begins, sharp enough to walk along. What made the edge is argued over; that it moves, slowly, eastwards or westwards depending on the decade, is not.`,

  'mantis-coast': `The eastern shore beyond Erosia, named for the hook-legged things that hunt its tidal flats. Ships water here when they must and do not stay the night.`,

  'supcliffe': `A cliff-top town on Erosia’s eastern coast, its harbour reached by a stair cut down the rock face. Everything that lands there is carried up by hand, which keeps the trade small and the town honest.`,

  'battle-of-mosscairn': `A field in central Erosia marked by the cairn that gave the fight its name, where an Order army caught a Chaos column in the open and broke it. The cairn is older than the battle; the battle is why anyone remembers the cairn.`,

  'sap-volcanoes': `A line of vents in western Erosia that erupt not lava but resin, throwing out amber flows that cool into hills. The amber traps whatever it catches, and the Erosians mine it for the things preserved inside.

An eruption can bury a valley in a week. It can also make whoever owns that valley extremely rich, which is why the settlements keep going back.`,

  'widdershins': `A ruined town in eastern Erosia, laid out in a spiral running the wrong way about. The design was supposed to ward something off. Whether it failed or succeeded is not recorded, only that the town is empty.`,

  'colostrum': `A town on Erosia’s eastern coast, sat on springs that run thick and white and are said to heal. The claim brings pilgrims, the pilgrims bring money, and Colostrum has built walls with it.`,

  'lake-serf': `A small lake in southern Erosia, worked by the villages around it under an obligation to the old Erosian crown that nobody has got round to abolishing. The fish are good and the arrangement is resented.`,

  'gushing-rapids': `The white water where the Erosian rivers drop off the highland to the southern coast, fast enough to run a mill on and far too fast to navigate. The portage road around them is the only land route east, and it is tolled.`,

  'oakenbrow-seed': `A Sylvaneth glade of the Oakenbrow, planted in southern Erosia where the old forest still holds. Oakenbrow are the most conservative of the great glades, slow to move and slower to forgive, and they regard the Free Cities’ timber trade as theft.

The seed is a border post as much as a grove. Anyone cutting east of it is cutting where the Sylvaneth have said they may not.`,

  // --- Invidia -------------------------------------------------------------
  'invidia': `The poisoned eastern continent, taken by Nurgle early and never taken back. Its rivers are irrigation channels for a garden, its hills are spoil heaps, and its people, where there are any, serve the powers that farm them.

Invidia is the staging ground for everything that comes west into the Swathe. An Order army that could hold even a coastal strip of it would change the whole war, which is why several have tried.`,

  'etiolated-coast': `Invidia’s northern shore, bleached pale by whatever runs out of the interior into the sea. The beaches are white, the water is clear, and nothing has lived in either for a long time.`,

  'sickling-sea': `The northern sea off Invidia, thin and sour and carrying whatever the continent drains into it. Ships cross it under sail and do not put their crews over the side for any reason.`,

  'bleeding-gate': `A monolith on Invidia’s eastern coast that weeps continuously, standing where a realmgate used to open. Whether the gate is dead or merely shut is contested by everyone with an interest in what is on the other side.

Nurgle’s servants keep it garrisoned. That alone tells the Order commanders on the far shore that it is worth something.`,

  'minuet-rivers': `A fan of small watercourses across central Invidia, stepping down the plain in an order so regular it looks designed. Gardeners of a sort did design them, and they are maintained.`,

  'princes-contention': `Broken ground in western Invidia where two Rotbringer lords have been fighting the same war over the same valleys for longer than any living mortal. Neither will win; Nurgle finds the contest pleasing, and keeps both supplied.`,

  'mannfreds-gambit': `A stretch of eastern Invidia where the Mortarch of Night marched an army in and marched most of it out again, having got whatever he came for. Nobody on the Chaos side will say what was taken, which is itself informative.

The ground is still littered with the dead of both hosts, and both sides have since found reasons to come back and look at them.`,

  'claim-of-horticulous': `The tract of Invidia that Horticulous Slimux has staked as his own plot, tended personally when he is in the realm and by his gardeners when he is not. What grows there is meant to grow there.

The Slaughterer of Cygor’s Vale takes cultivation seriously. Armies that trample his plantings acquire a personal enemy who will follow them across a realm to discuss it.`,

  'the-reality-sores': `A cluster of thin places in southern Invidia where the realm has worn through and something else shows underneath. They are marked on the plate because they move, and knowing where they were last season is better than nothing.`,

  'splitskin-peaks': `A cracked range across Invidia’s southern middle, the rock flaking off in sheets. The passes are climbable and the climbing kills a proportion of anyone who tries, which is the only defence the range provides.`,

  'castrominus-sludge': `A slow flow of semi-solid filth down Invidia’s eastern flank, moving perhaps a mile a year and swallowing everything in its path. Settlements ahead of it have a generation’s warning and mostly use it.`,

  'toxic-irrigations': `The maintained channel system of southern Invidia, carrying the continent’s poisons out to where they are wanted. It is engineering, not accident, and the engineers are still at work.`,

  'clotted-choke': `The strait between Invidia and the western sea, silted and weeded until only a narrow lane remains open. Whoever holds the lane holds the only fast passage into the Greenhaunch Sea.`,

  'gryst': `A grit-choked channel between the islets off Invidia’s south-western coast, shallow, sharp-bottomed and navigable by people who know it. Pilots from the local villages make a living from that knowledge and guard it.`,

  'nothingwell': `A shaft sunk into the Invidian plain that has never reached water and has never reached bottom. Things are thrown down it as offerings. Nothing comes back up, which the local cults consider proof of something.`,

  'greenhaunch-sea': `The wide green-tinged sea between Invidia and the southern continents, thick with weed that fouls a rudder within a day. Fleets crossing it carry crews whose only job is cutting the hull clear.`,

  'the-vertebrae': `A chain of reef-islets running south from Invidia in a line too regular to be accidental, each one the size of a hill. Sailors call them the spine of something and do not like to look at them long.`,

  'sliming-woad': `Cultivated Invidian ground where a crop is grown for its dye and its rot in equal measure. The woad is shipped west and sold to people who do not ask where it was grown.`,

  'grimscale-peaks': `A scaled grey range on Invidia’s eastern coast, the rock breaking into plates that shed off the slopes. Nothing crosses it; the coast road goes round, and the coast road is therefore worth holding.`,

  'hind-sea': `The far eastern water beyond the Grimscale Peaks, at the back of everything, sailed by almost nobody. What is on the other side of it is a matter of rumour rather than charts.`,

  // --- Neos ----------------------------------------------------------------
  'neos': `Young country on the southern side of the Swathe, land that has come up out of the sea within recorded memory and is still growing. Nothing here is old, which in Ghyran is remarkable enough to draw scholars, settlers and the Sylvaneth alike.

The soil is clean. That is the whole of Neos’s value and the whole of its danger: it is the one large tract of uncorrupted ground within reach of the Jadewound, and everyone can see it.`,

  'jadewound': `A gash of blighted jungle where Nurgle’s rot first took hold and never fully released its grip, the canopy sickly green even where nothing else grows wrong. Stormcast and Sylvaneth patrol its edge in shifts, less to reclaim it than to keep whatever festers there from spreading further.`,

  'everdusk': `A twilight forest where Ghyran’s endless growth slows just enough for shadow to gather beneath the canopy, neither wholly wholesome nor wholly corrupted. Sylvaneth kindreds treat it as contested ground even among themselves, wary of what its half-light might yet decide to become.`,

  'congelush-veldt': `Open grassland in western Neos where the dew freezes most nights and thaws by mid-morning, leaving the grass bright and the ground hard. It is the best riding country in the southern Swathe and the only ground here an army can deploy properly.`,

  'lamenter-peaks': `A range on Neos’s southern coast that sounds, in certain winds, like a crowd of people grieving. The effect is acoustic and the explanation does not help anyone who has to camp there.`,

  'greencloud-bay': `A bay on the southern Neos coast permanently under a low green haze blown off the Jadewound. Ships anchor in it because it is sheltered, and their crews come out of it coughing.`,

  'amber-littoral': `The southern shore of Neos, where amber washes up in quantity after a storm. Gathering rights are held by the coastal villages and disputed by everybody else, regularly and violently.`,

  'dirtwoad': `Worked ground on the eastern edge of Neos, cropped for dye and root vegetables by settlers who arrived within living memory. It is ordinary, productive farmland, which this far east makes it extraordinary.`,

  'rotwoad': `The southern continuation of the same dye country, further gone: the crop still grows but the ground under it has begun to turn. The farmers are still farming it, on the grounds that the alternative is leaving.`,

  // --- Quogmia -------------------------------------------------------------
  'quogmia': `A sprawling mire of stagnant channels and overgrown ruins, too wet for Nurgle’s rot to fully claim and too poisoned for Ghyran’s usual abundance to cure. Trappers and smugglers use its waterways to move goods nobody wants inspected, and the mire keeps most of their secrets.`,

  'gnarlwood': `A dense, ill-tempered forest of ancient trees predating most of Ghyran’s other woodlands, its groves said to hold grudges the way duardin hold grudge-books. Sylvaneth wardens rarely welcome visitors, and the wood itself seems to agree with them.`,

  'dreamloss-realmgate': `A realmgate on Quogmia’s northern edge, named for what it takes from those who use it. The transit is safe; what arrives on the far side has lost something it cannot name and will not get back.

It is used anyway, because it is the fastest way out of the southern mire, and because the toll is one most travellers do not notice until much later.`,

  'blackpyre': `A burning mound at the head of the Quogmian mire, alight since before the Age of Chaos and fed by nothing anyone can identify. Cults gather at it. So do refugees, because it is the only reliable dry heat in the south.`,

  'spire-glacis': `A settlement on the slope below a single stone spire on Quogmia’s northern march, the town walls run up against the rock so the spire serves as a keep. It is the northernmost place in the mire that can properly be called defended.`,

  'healers-folly': `The channel running down into Quogmia, named for a physician who believed the mire’s waters could be cleaned and who spent a life proving otherwise. The channel is the main route into the interior and carries most of what goes in or out.`,

  'lake-trog': `A black lake on Quogmia’s western edge, deep, cold and inhabited. Troggoths come out of it in numbers when the water is disturbed, so the villages around it have learned not to disturb the water.`,

  'troggoth-isles': `A scatter of hummocks in the western mire where the troggoths of Lake Trog haul out to sleep. Bounty parties go in for the hides and come back short-handed often enough to keep the price high.`,

  'pested-caves': `A cave system in the Quogmian hills, colonised by vermin on a scale that has made the caves a landmark rather than a nuisance. Skaven work the deeper galleries; what works the shallow ones is worse-tempered and less organised.`,

  'the-great-shear': `A cliff line running across eastern Quogmia where the land has simply broken and dropped, leaving a face a hundred feet high and leagues long. There are three ways up it. All three are held.`,

  'ogor-hinterlands': `Open mire-country in southern Quogmia claimed by ogor tribes who follow the mournfang herds through it. They tax caravans by eating a proportion of them, and consider this a reasonable and long-established custom.

Nobody has driven the ogors out, and the tribes that have tried are remembered by the ogors as provisions.`,

  'sea-of-blades': `The narrow sea between Quogmia and the southern forests, its surface broken by upright reefs sharp enough to open a hull. It is crossable in a flat calm and lethal otherwise.`,

  'thrall-point': `A headland on the eastern Quogmian coast where slave galleys have watered for generations, and where the barracoons still stand. It changes hands between raiders rather than between kingdoms, and its use has never changed at all.`,

  'verdural-forests': `Old timber on the southern island beyond Quogmia, untouched because getting a log off the island is harder than growing a new one anywhere else. The Sylvaneth are content with that arithmetic and take steps to keep it true.`,

  'ochre-cliffs': `The red-brown cliffs along the southern island’s coast, sheer for miles and pierced by a handful of landing beaches. The beaches are the only way in, which makes the island easy to hold and difficult to supply.`,

  // --- Humidia -------------------------------------------------------------
  'humidia': `The southern reach of the Swathe, a country of standing heat and permanent rain where the air itself is work to breathe. Things rot here faster than anywhere else in Ghyran, including armies.

Humidia is not fought over for what it has but for what it lies across: the sea road south and the only land route between the western isles and the Quogmian mire.`,

  'drug-throat': `The strait between Humidia and the Squelchlobe coast, named for the cargo that has always moved through it. Both shores make their living off that traffic, and neither has any interest in policing it.`,

  'hernia-promontory': `A bulge of land pushing west out of Humidia, half of it slumped into the sea and the rest going the same way. Settlements on it are temporary by design.`,

  'scab-tongue': `A crusted spit on the Humidian coast, hard on top and soft underneath. It is the only place a ship can lie alongside for leagues, and every crew that uses it does so knowing what is under the crust.`,

  'shimmerfalls-of-gloriphus': `A waterfall on the Humidian escarpment that throws light rather than merely reflecting it, said to be the work of an Age of Myth wizard whose name is all that survives of him. Pilgrims come to stand in the spray.

The falls sit on the only pass up the escarpment. The pilgrim road and the military road are the same road, which has made Gloriphus a place of worship and a chokepoint at once.`,

  'threadwyrm-river': `The river draining southern Humidia, braided into so many channels that no two charts of it agree. Local pilots are indispensable and know it.`,

  'behemates-gnaw': `The channel bitten out of Humidia’s southern coast, a bay so regular in outline that the name is a description rather than a metaphor. Whatever did the biting is not in the bay now.`,

  // --- Yska ----------------------------------------------------------------
  'yska': `The southern grassland kingdom, dry by Ghyran’s standards and therefore healthy, its people living off herds rather than crops. Yska is the only large tract in the southern Swathe that Nurgle’s work has not reached, and the Yskari are entirely aware of what that makes them.

Riders hold the veldt and the Free Cities hold nothing here at all. Every Dawnbringer column sent south to change that has come back or not come back; none of them has founded a city.`,

  'hardship': `A ruined settlement on Yska’s northern coast, sat on the one promontory in a hundred miles with fresh water. The water is still there and the settlement is not, which the Yskari take as a lesson about building where the sea can reach you.`,

  'the-birth-scars': `A belt of raw gullies across northern Yska where the land tore open as it rose, still bare rock and still widening. The gullies run the wrong way for travel and have broken up more than one invasion that tried to cross them at speed.`,

  'ysrian-veldt': `The open heart of the Yskari grassland, grazed by the herds the kingdom lives on and crossed by the riders who follow them. There is nothing here to fortify and nothing here to besiege, which has defeated better-equipped armies than it has any right to.`,

  'the-lackslough': `A dry marsh in eastern Yska, a slough that has lost its water and kept its ground, cracked into plates a wagon can cross. It is the fastest route across the kingdom and the first thing to flood when the rains come early.`,

  'greenhill-massacre': `A low hill in central Yska where a column of settlers was caught in the open and killed to the last, and where the Yskari clans and the Free Cities each blame the other. The hill is marked on the plate because both sides insist it be marked.

It is also the obvious muster ground for the whole central veldt, and both sides use it, separately and with an eye on each other.`,

  'touchwood': `A Yskari town grown up around the one stand of timber on the open veldt, guarded so closely that taking a branch is a capital matter. In a treeless grassland, the wood is worth more than the herds.`,

  'southerncrust': `A town on the hard-baked southern edge of the Yskari plain, built where the crust is thick enough to carry stone. It is the kingdom’s only walled settlement and the place the clans withdraw to when withdrawing becomes necessary.`,

  'chanters-cliffs': `Sea cliffs along Yska’s south-western coast that sing in an onshore wind, loud enough to be heard inland. The Yskari read weather in the pitch, and ships offshore read their position by it.`,

  'the-southern-seas': `The water between Yska and the southern islands, shallow, warm and thick with life. It is the easiest sailing in the Swathe, which means it is also where the raiders are.`,

  'tendril-reach': `The weed-choked eastern arm of the Tendril Sea, reaching up towards the Yskari shore. Craft that go in under oar come out under tow, if they come out.`,

  // --- Decrepita -----------------------------------------------------------
  'decrepita': `The drowned western archipelago, all that is left of a kingdom the sea has been taking back for an age. Ruins stand in shallow water for miles offshore, and the islanders live on what is above the tideline this decade.

Salvage is the whole economy. What comes up out of the old Decrepitan halls is old enough to be worth a great deal to the right buyer, and the diving is done by people with no other options.`,

  'naiad-archipelago': `A chain of small islands running south-east from Decrepita, each one a rock with a village on it. The channels between them are the only sheltered route down the western coast, and the islanders pilot it for a fee.`,

  'slaughters-haven': `The strait between the Naiad islands and the mainland, an anchorage so good that fleets have repeatedly met in it and fought over it. The name predates the most recent occasion.`,

  'sanctum-isle': `An island off the south-western shore holding a shrine old enough that nobody agrees which power it was raised to. It is respected by all sides, which makes it the standing venue for parleys nobody trusts.`,

  'mortis-isles': `A scatter of bare rocks in the far south-west used as a burial ground by the whole western archipelago. The dead are brought out by boat and left above the tideline, and the islands are otherwise uninhabited.`,

  'tendril-sea': `The great southern sea below the archipelago, thick with drifting weed that gathers into mats big enough to walk on and rots from beneath. Ships keep to the open lanes and the lanes move.`,

  // --- Kurnotheal ----------------------------------------------------------
  'kurnotheal': `The far south-eastern forest country, held by the Kurnothi — the horned kindreds of Ghyran who answer to Kurnoth rather than to Alarielle, and who have never accepted that the distinction is unimportant. They allow no settlement and very few visitors.

What the Kurnothi guard here they have not explained. Every expedition sent to find out has been turned back at the tree line, politely the first time.`,

  'heartwood-coast': `The eastern shore of Kurnotheal, timber right down to the waterline and not a single harbour cut into any of it. Ships that want water here go ashore by boat and ask permission.`,

  'undying-tract': `A stand of Kurnotheal forest where nothing dies on schedule — trees felled in it put out leaf where they lie, and so, occasionally, do other things. The Kurnothi walk it freely and warn outsiders away.`,

  'neodine-canal': `A cut through the low ground between the Greenhaunch Sea and the southern water, old enough that its builders are unknown and deep enough to still be in use. Whoever holds both ends can close the whole southern sea route.`,

  'lurkers-cove': `A hidden inlet on Kurnotheal’s western coast, invisible from seaward until a ship is nearly inside it. Raiders have used it for generations, which the Kurnothi tolerate for reasons they have not shared.`,

  // --- The Amnios Sea and the southern shore -------------------------------
  'amnios-sea': `The great southern ocean at the bottom of the Swathe, warm, still and close to featureless. Crossing it is a matter of provisioning rather than seamanship, and the crossing takes weeks.`,

  'ellipsis-isles': `A ring of low islands in the Amnios Sea, laid out in an almost perfect oval around water that is deeper inside the ring than outside it. Nobody has satisfactorily explained the arrangement and several expeditions have tried.`,

  'the-fleeing-siblings': `Two islands in the eastern Amnios Sea, separated by a narrow channel and drifting apart by a measurable distance each year. The local name says they are running from something; the local name does not say what.`,

  'pensids-gamble': `A settlement on the southern shore founded by a captain who wagered everything on a coastline nobody had charted and was, narrowly, right. It is the only harbour on the polar coast and the last stop before the ice.`,

  'ymbolqui-lakes': `A chain of cold lakes along the southern shore, frozen at the edges most of the year and never entirely so at the centre. The fishing supports a string of villages and nothing else.`,

  'wintercoast': `The southern polar shore of the Swathe, where Ghyran’s growth finally stops. It is the one stretch of the realm where nothing is spreading, rotting or reaching, and to certain exhausted refugees from the north that has been reason enough to settle it.`,
};
